.Dd $Mdocdate: August 3 2026 $
.Dt FuguLib::Random 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm FuguLib::Random
.Nd random bytes and random passwords
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use FuguLib::Random;

my $nonce = FuguLib::Random->random_bytes(12);
my $password = FuguLib::Random->random_password(32);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
gives random bytes from
.Pa /dev/urandom
and random passwords built from them.
.Pp
Every method is a class method.
The module keeps no state, never logs, and uses core Perl only.
A programming error dies.
There is no partial result and no quiet fallback, because a caller
cannot recover from a secret with a known prefix.
.Ss random_bytes
.Fn random_bytes "$length"
returns
.Fa $length
bytes from
.Pa /dev/urandom .
.Pp
The method dies when the device does not open, and dies on a short
read.
Neither one has a recovery: a caller that continues with fewer bytes
than it asked for builds a secret with a known prefix.
.Ss random_password
.Fn random_password "$length"
returns a random password of exactly
.Fa $length
characters.
The default length is 32.
.Pp
The alphabet is URL-safe base64, so the password survives a shell, a
URL and a configuration file with no quoting.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn random_bytes
and
.Fn random_password
return a string of the asked-for length.
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn random_bytes
dies when
.Pa /dev/urandom
does not open, on a short read, and on a length that is not positive.
.Fn random_password
dies on a length that is not positive.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arc4random 3 ,
.Xr random 4
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn random_password
trims a base64 string to the asked-for length.
A password of
.Fa n
characters therefore carries about six bits of entropy for each
character, and not eight.
.Pp
The HAP protocol library has its own randomness in
.Vt Protocol::HAP::Crypto .
The duplication is deliberate: the protocol library stays
self-contained across a distribution boundary.
